  "language": "english",
  "text": "This is definitely going to be a gotcha video. I've been waiting for somebody to make this comment for like a year. And I'll tell you why, because this is flawed logic. And basically the logic goes something like this. You can't make models to beat Vegas because Vegas makes models to predict games. So it's conflicting. It argues for being able to use models to predict outcomes by Vegas being able to do it, while simultaneously arguing that models can't predict outcomes, right? It's the same idea. He's saying you can't fight fire with fire. They have way more resource and all these other things. I just did a 10-minute video explaining why Vegas is comfortable doing this. You are 100% right that they have teams. Some, some, not all, but they put up a number. And that number is not a guess. It is an informed guess. It is a pretty crude model that they throw up and start the quote-unquote opening market based off that price, rather than just opening it at some random point. Now doing this allows them to get to the closing line quicker by putting up a more informed number that the market then can take from there. But by no way is what Vegas does. They are in the business of getting to the closing line using the market information as fast and as cheap as possible. They are not in the business of trying to bet against all comers at all times. Now the retail books have decided to make a business model, which is we're willing to take on all retail clientele and they literally kick out winners. Market makers are willing to take a bet from anybody at their set limits, which makes them unique, which is why professionals bet there in the first place. But don't get it wrong. They are playing a different game than you. They're laying plus 110. You're laying minus 110. You have to do a lot more work to overcome the big. They have to do a lot less work. They can pretty much rely on a market dynamic and make money. But the other thing I would want to just point out and challenge everybody is, guys, go look up the earnings reports for FanDuel, DraftKings, which is Flutter Entertainment, Penn, who owns ESPN, BetMGM, Caesars. You can see their profits. You can see their holds. They have to report this stuff. Guys, the average hold on the book right now for FanDuel and DraftKings is 8%. I'm not saying that that's not a lot or that that is not a profitable business, but if you look at who's actually profitable right now, Flutter or FanDuel is one of the only larger books that's actually making money. Everybody else is losing money because they spend it all on advertising to acquire new customers. The reality, guys, is this is not some sexy business where you just can print unlimited money. The hold is the business on the book side. That's what their business is. Their business isn't predicting the future and taking bets from all people. There's very few situations where this is going to be profitable, and they rather use the professional information on their side to inform their lines rather than try to beat all those people. Hope this helps.",
